;; spec:   §5, §6 (types §6.6, §6.7, §8.2)
;; expect: accept
;; result: 9
;; audit:  clean
;; A weak reference to an immutable array held only by a let cell, then
;; a unique write through the cell. The decided rules give 9 (fibref
;; agrees), but they contradict other statements of the spec; this case
;; pins the behaviour and records the contradiction.
;;   types §6.6: "Unique write (Decided, §5): array-set! and set-field!
;;   read the place's content without retaining, test fib.unique? (§8.2:
;;   the flags have none of SHARED, IMMORTAL, STACK, and the count is
;;   exactly 1), and either write the object in place or build a copy".
;; HAS-WEAK is not among the flags tested, so the array (count 1 once the
;; temporary @c of (weak @c) is released) is written in place, and the
;; weak reference, taken of the array [0], now upgrades to [9]:
;;   ownership.md §1: "Objects are immutable unless they are a cell";
;;   ownership.md §5: "So a caller that is still using the old value (an
;;   iteration, another binding) keeps seeing the old value";
;;   ownership.md §6: "(deref w) returns a strong reference, or nil once
;;   the object is gone".
;; Under the counting semantics without the in-place reuse, the write
;; makes a new array, the old one [0] is freed when the cell lets go of
;; it, and (deref w) is nil (result 100). So the unique-write
;; optimisation, which §2 presents as unobservable ("The compiler must
;; produce the same results ... just with fewer count operations"), is
;; observable through a weak reference, and an "immutable" object is seen
;; to change. Memory stays safe (a crossing marks the target SHARED,
;; §5.5, so no other thread can watch it change).
;;
;; Observed (fibref run):
;;   result: 9
;;   audit:  clean=true leak-cycles=0 leaks=0 errors=0
;; Diagnosis: spec. Either fib.unique? must also refuse objects with
;; HAS-WEAK (then the result is 100 in both implementations), or
;; ownership.md §1/§6 must say that a weak reference follows in-place
;; updates of its target.
(defun main () -> i64
  (let ((c (cell (array 1 0)))
        (w (weak @c)))
    (do (array-set! &c 0 9)
        (match @w ((some a) (array-get a 0)) (nil 100)))))
